---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import Cloud from "reicon/icons/Cloud";
import Rocket from "reicon/icons/Rocket";

const installCommand = "curl -fsSL https://cdn.coollabs.io/coolify/install.sh | sudo bash";

const faq = [
  {
    question: "Is Coolify a free Heroku alternative?",
    answer:
      "Self-hosted Coolify has no license fee. It is open source under the Apache-2.0 license. You pay for the Linux server. Coolify Cloud is optional and paid. It runs the dashboard, and your apps still run on servers you connect.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does Coolify deploy when I push to GitHub?",
    answer:
      "Yes. A GitHub App, GitHub Actions, or a repository webhook can start a deployment when you push. GitLab, Bitbucket, and Gitea are supported sources too. Coolify does not use a Heroku Git remote.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I run databases and backups?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Coolify can run PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, MongoDB, Redis, Dragonfly, KeyDB, ClickHouse, or SQLite on your server, with scheduled backups where the engine supports them. You operate the database.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does Coolify issue HTTPS certificates?",
    answer:
      "Add the domain with `https://`. Traefik or Caddy requests and renews the certificate. Ports 80 and 443 must be open on the server.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="What Are the Best Free Heroku Alternatives? | Coolify"
  canonicalUrl="https://coolify.io/free-heroku-alternative"
  description="Coolify is a free, open-source Heroku alternative (Apache-2.0). You pay for the server, not a license. Git deploys, databases, backups, and HTTPS."
>
  <section class="px-4">
    <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-fg md:text-5xl">
      What are the best free Heroku alternatives<span class="text-warning">?</span>
    </h1>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-3xl px-4 text-left">
    <Prose>
      <p class="text-base md:text-lg">
        <strong>Short answer:</strong> Coolify is free, open-source software under the Apache-2.0 license, and you
        install it on a server you provide. There is no Coolify license fee. You still pay for that server, and
        <a href="/cloud">Coolify Cloud</a> is the paid option only when you want the Coolify team to run the dashboard.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="card mt-4 py-3 px-4">
      <code class="block overflow-x-auto font-mono text-sm whitespace-nowrap text-fg select-all">
        <span class="pr-2 text-fg-faint select-none">$</span>{installCommand}
      </code>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-4">
      <h2>What free means</h2>
      <p>
        Self-hosted Coolify has no license fee. The
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/selfhosted-cloud-comparison">self-hosted and Cloud comparison</a>
        says all current and upcoming features are included. You pay your server provider for the machine that runs
        Coolify and your apps. Server size and the install steps are in the
        <a href="/deploy-apps-on-your-own-vps">VPS guide</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Coolify Cloud is optional. The same docs list it at $5 per month for up to two connected servers, plus $3 per
        month for each server after that. Pricing can change. See
        <a href="/pricing">pricing</a>. In both cases your apps, databases, and services run on servers you connect.
        Cloud does not host those workloads.
      </p>

      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Deploys from Git. On GitHub, a GitHub App, GitHub Actions, or a repository webhook can
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/sources/github/auto-deploy">deploy when you push</a>.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/sources/gitlab">GitLab</a>,
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/sources/bitbucket">Bitbucket</a>, and
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/sources/gitea">Gitea</a> are supported sources too.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/sources/overview">Sources</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Databases on your server, including PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, MongoDB, Redis, Dragonfly, KeyDB, ClickHouse,
          and SQLite, with scheduled backups where the engine supports them.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases">Databases</a>.
          <a href="/self-host-postgres-with-backups">Postgres with backups</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Automatic HTTPS. Add the domain with <code>https://</code>. Traefik or Caddy requests and renews the
          certificate. Open ports 80 and 443.
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/core/networking/domains">Domains</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Builds with <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/builds/nixpacks/overview">Nixpacks</a>,
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/builds/railpack">Railpack</a>, a Dockerfile, Compose, or a
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/builds/static">static site</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/applications/deployments/preview-deployments">Preview deployments</a>
          for pull requests on GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, and Gitea.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/services">One-click services</a> on a server you connect.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The license is <a href="https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/main/LICENSE">Apache-2.0</a>. For a sourced
        comparison with other self-hosted tools, see
        <a href="/compare/coolify-vs-dokploy-vs-caprover-vs-dokku-vs-kamal">Coolify vs Dokploy vs CapRover vs Dokku vs Kamal</a>.
        Heroku's own pages are the source for Heroku facts on the
        <a href="/compare/heroku-alternative">Heroku comparison</a>. Static sites and previews are on the
        <a href="/netlify-alternative">Netlify alternatives</a> page.
      </p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <Faq items={faq} />
    </div>

    <section class="card mt-14 p-6 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-fg">Get started<span class="text-warning">.</span></h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-xl text-sm text-fg-dim">
        Install Coolify on a server you already have. If you would rather not run the dashboard, Coolify Cloud hosts
        it. Your apps stay on your servers.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/start-with-self-hosted" class="btn btn-primary">
          <Icon icon={Rocket} class="size-4" />
          Start with self-hosted
        </a>
        <a href="/pricing" class="btn btn-neutral">
          <Icon icon={Cloud} class="size-4" />
          Try Coolify Cloud
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
